    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID39",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q52be96733daa503c-chunk_4f6584444a96170df0971b59",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>1.2 incitement and encouragement of harm</strong> – Inciting or encouraging an individual, group, or general audience to harm a woman or group of women. It includes language where the author seeks to rationalise and/or justify harming women to another person.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p>2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls &gt; 2.1. State of the art <sup>(1)</sup> &gt; Online hate speech / incitement to violence or hatred<br><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Hate speech is a broad term referring to all types of conduct publicly inciting violence or hatred directed against a group of people or member of such a group defined by reference to race, colour, religion, descent or national or ethnic origin (Council of the European Union, 2008).\n\nWhile hate speech online is not intrinsically different from similar expressions found offline, there are peculiar challenges unique to online content and its regulation related to its permanence, itinerancy, anonymity and cross-jurisdictional character (Gagliardone et al., 2015). Moreover, full-fledged hate speech campaigns often take place online, when the same victim or group of victims is simultaneously targeted by multiple perpetrators.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q52be96733daa503c-chunk_4f6584444a96170df0971b59",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
        "DataVisibility": {
