    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID55",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Qeb30212bcbb2a6d9-chunk_fd0e89195cea83dab4c6d44e",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.3 dehumanising attacks and overt sexual objectification</strong> – Derogating women by comparing them to non-human entities such as vermin, disease or refuse, or overtly reducing them to sexual objects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p>What is sexual harassment?<br><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Some of the sexist behaviour listed in the Council of Europe recommendation on combating sexism, such as ‘derogatory comments, objectification, sexist humour’ can be categorised as sexual harassment as defined under Article 12a of the Staff Regulations.\n\nAbout a third of women who faced sexual harassment in the EU experienced it in the workplace.\n\nAdditionally, any ongoing pattern of sexist behaviour can create an environment that is ‘intimidating, hostile, offensive or disturbing,’ as defined under Article 12a of the Staff Regulations.\n\nSuch behaviour is also illegal under several EU directives and prohibited under the Council of Europe Convention on Preventing and Combating Violence against Women and Domestic Violence (the Istanbul Convention). EU law distinguishes between sex-based harassment, which is any unwanted behaviour directed to someone because they are a woman or a man, and sexual harassment, which involves unwanted conduct of a sexual nature. Sex-based harassment includes sexual harassment and some sexist behaviour.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Qeb30212bcbb2a6d9-chunk_fd0e89195cea83dab4c6d44e",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
